  "query_preview": "..Quiet weather is forecast around northern Arizona for \nthe remainder of the holiday weekend and through Monday. A couple of \nshortwaves will brush the area, one on Saturday through CO/NM and \nthe next on Monday coming in from the west. These both look dry for \nnorthern Arizona with some mid/high clouds at times and some \nincreased winds. Winds on Saturday will be W/NW with gusts 20-30 mph \nmainly Mogollon Rim/White Mtns northward. Monday's winds will be SW \nwith gusts 20-30 mph area wide. Daytime temperatures through Monday \nwill be close to 10 degrees warmer than late November averages.\n\nTuesday through Wednesday - a stronger low pressure system is \nforecast to impact the state, with the potential for significant \nprecipitation including some of the first snow of the season down to \n7000 feet or so, and another threat of impactful rises on area \ncreeks and rivers. However, the first impact will be gusty southwest \nwinds on Tuesday with a good chance of advisory level gusts hitting \n40-50 mph. Current timing of the system looks to bring precipitation \nin from west to east starting Tuesday evening with the potential for \nthe heaviest overnight through the day Wednesday. The mean forecast \nprecipitation amounts from the latest NBM are in the 1.00\"-2.00\" \nlocally 2.50\" range from the Mogollon Rim/White Mtns south into \nGila/Yavapai counties. The heaviest precipitation is forecast in the \nfavored upslope zones along the rim and Bradshaw Mtns. There is \nstill uncertainty in the track/strength of the low as it moves \nthrough, and precipitation amounts higher or lower than those \nmentioned are possible. If the mean forecast amounts or higher \noccur, we are looking at a threat of significant rises on area \ncreeks/rivers by Wednesday into Thursday. This threat depends \nsomewhat on snow levels as well, as lower snow levels down to \n6500/7000 feet reduce the amount of runoff coming from the Mogollon \nRim. Currently this does not look like a particularly cold storm, so \nsnow levels don't fall to the 7000 ft level until much of the \nheavier precipitation is over, but this bears watching as it will \nhave impacts on winter travel conditions at higher elevations as \nwell as the flood threat at lower elevations. The latest WPC \nExcessive Rainfall Outlook includes a Marginal Risk from the \nMogollon Rim down into Yavapai/Gila counties for the Tuesday night \nperiod and this may expand for Wednesday.",
